comments[3]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-01T11:09:06.308Z","harness:claude-code","Implementation: parseCsvKv (src/core/item/parse.ts) now treats a segment as a continuation of the active continuable value (text, note, body, ...) when the token before its first = or : does not match the new CSV_KEY_PATTERN [A-Za-z0-9_.-]+, which is the same key shape the markdown-line parser already requires. \"text=(a, b) c. Related: d\" now parses to one text value. An identifier-shaped token still opens a key, so the GH-258 typo detection (lable=main) is unchanged. In parseLogSeed (src/sdk/lifecycle/create.ts), the unsupported-key fallback still records the whole entry, but it strips a leading text= or text: opener, so a seed such as \"text=hello, world: big\", whose post-comma token is a valid identifier, no longer stores the syntax as prose. Tests: parse-utils.spec.ts (continuation and typo cases) and context-integrity-recovery.spec.ts (three log-seed shapes).\n"
  "2026-11-01T11:26:15.978Z","harness:claude-code","Scope correction after the full suite: the log-seed text= stripping was reverted. create-command.spec (\"accepts intuitive rich agent seed inputs without dropping metadata\", GH-1149 lineage) deliberately records the raw entry losslessly when a seed carries an identifier-shaped unknown key such as source=dogfood. Removing the opener would change what the agent wrote in exactly the ambiguous case. The parser continuation fix alone resolves the reproduced defect: \"text=(a, b) c. Related: d\" now parses as pure text. An empty key (note=alpha,=beta) still refuses as before. The documented rule is now: inside a prose value, only an identifier-shaped token followed by = or : opens a new key. Quote the value (text=\"hello, world: big\") to embed such a token. src/sdk/lifecycle/create.ts was unlinked from this item.\n"
  "2026-11-01T15:40:56.290Z","harness:claude-code","Ordering note (PR 1648 review): gate_evidence was recorded after close and release because the defect-evidence gate first failed on the hosted run. It describes the shipped fix and its guards and is a follow-up record; the append-only history is unchanged."
